Aggregate copy at field offset >= 64 (TRISC codegen)
=====================================================

Pinned 2026-04-29. `emitAddImm(destReg, baseReg, offset)` for an
out-of-immediate-range offset was emitting

```
movi r3, 64
add  r4, r3, r3   ; <-- baseReg clobbered before use
```

when called as `emitAddImm(4, 3, 64)`. The temp-register pick
only avoided `destReg`, so `tmp == baseReg == 3` and the offset
load wiped the source pointer; `add` then doubled the offset
into r4, producing a wild address.

The bug only fired for aggregate field-by-field copies whose
offset crossed the 64-byte addi-immediate boundary. `StrBuilder`
inside a 10-field `TabWriter` (the trigger): `cell_buf` lives
at offset 72, and copying the constructed temp `TabWriter` into
the hidden-return slot used the by-byte copy loop with offsets
0, 8, ..., 56, 64, 72, ..., 112. Offsets <= 56 used `addi` and
worked; offsets >= 64 corrupted the source pointer, so the
*destination* wrote the right bytes (its offset path was fine)
but the *source* read garbage.

Fix: in `emitAddImm`, pick a temp that is neither `destReg` nor
`baseReg` (preferring r3, then r2, then r4, then r1).

The minimal repro mirrors the real-world structure that
surfaced the bug: a 10-field struct whose 9th field is itself a
struct that gets a single mutating call after construction.
Triggered all 3 std/text/tabwriter tests.

    module sysl.tests.aggregate_copy_offset_64

    import std.builder.{new_builder, StrBuilder}
    import std.debug.{assert}

    struct TabwriterCell
        text: string
        width: int
        htab: bool

    struct TabwriterLine
        cells: []TabwriterCell

    struct TabWriter
        out: StrBuilder
        minwidth: int
        tabwidth: int
        padding: int
        padchar: byte
        flags: int
        align_right: bool
        lines: []TabwriterLine
        cell_buf: StrBuilder
        widths: []int

    new_tab_writer() -> TabWriter
        var lines0 = (new [0]TabwriterLine)[:0]
        lines0 = append(lines0, TabwriterLine((new [0]TabwriterCell)[:0]))
        TabWriter(new_builder(), 0, 8, 0, byte(32), 0, false, lines0, new_builder(), (new [0]int)[:0])

    TabWriter.write_byte(b: byte)
        self.cell_buf.write_byte(b)

    #test
    test_aggregate_copy_offset_64_persists() -> unit
        var w = new_tab_writer()
        w.write_byte(byte('a'))
        assert(w.cell_buf.len() == 1, "one byte")
